<title>CANADA: Metro-Richelieu Norois beer gets okay.</title>
<headline>Metro-Richelieu Norois beer gets okay.</headline>
<dateline>MONTREAL 1996-11-08</dateline>
<text>
<p>Metro-Richelieu Inc said on Friday that the Supreme Court of Canada refused on Thursday to hear an appeal of a lower court ruling which allowed the company to sell its private label Norois beer.</p>
<p>Metro-Richelieu said L'Association des brasseurs du Quebec, which represents the Molson Cos' Molson-O'Keefe brands and the Labatt label of Interbrew SA, of Belgium, had appealed a Quebec Court of Appeal decision which had confirmed a Quebec government agency's ruling allowing Metro-Richelieu to market the Norois beer in its stores.</p>
<p>Since the grocery store chain's Norois beer was launched in July 1994, more than 35 million bottles have been sold, the company said.</p>
<p>-- Reuters Montreal bureau (514) 985-2434</p>
